<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<package xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/packaging/2013/05/nuspec.xsd">
  <metadata>
    <id>VL.Mapsui</id>
    <!-- The WORKING version: always one ahead of what is on nuget.org (0.0.1-alpha, 2026-09-26), so a
         local repack can never be mistaken for the published package in a NuGet cache. Managed in
         step with VL.NetTopologySuite, whose working version is the dependency below. -->
    <version>0.0.2-alpha</version>
    <title>VL.Mapsui — Mapsui map engine nodes for vvvv gamma</title>
    <authors>VL.Mapsui Contributors</authors>
    <owners>VL.Mapsui Contributors</owners>
    <requireLicenseAcceptance>false</requireLicenseAcceptance>
    <license type="expression">MIT</license>
    <projectUrl>https://github.com/rednotfound/VL.Mapsui</projectUrl>
    <!-- nuget.org shows this on the package page. The path is the path INSIDE the package (README.md
         is packed to docs\ below), and nuget pack refuses to pack if it is not in <files>. Settled
         the same way in VL.NetTopologySuite, 2026-09-25. -->
    <readme>docs\README.md</readme>
    <description>
      EARLY - not ready for real work yet.

      A real map engine as nodes for vvvv gamma. Tile layers from OpenStreetMap or any XYZ
      service, your own geometry drawn on top, labels taken from your data, and a Pick node
      that tells you which feature you are pointing at.

      Deliberately not one all-in-one map node. The pieces are Mapsui's own - Map, layers,
      Navigator - so a patch composes them, decides for itself how the mouse drives the map,
      and can put its own layers on it. That is what a patching environment is for.

      Geometry crosses the boundary as NetTopologySuite, so whatever produced it - a file, a
      service, VL.NetTopologySuite, a ForEach over a record of your own - never has to know that
      Mapsui will draw it.

      32 nodes and 19 help patches, from the smallest working map to styling by a value; every
      node opens one on F1.

      The node surface can still change between prereleases, and Mapsui's editing and WMS/WFS
      surfaces are not wrapped yet. Requires vvvv gamma 7.4 or newer (.NET 8).
      Install with: nuget install VL.Mapsui -pre
    </description>
    <summary>Mapsui map engine as composable nodes for vvvv gamma.</summary>
    <releaseNotes>
      0.0.2-alpha - In development, not published.

      0.0.1-alpha (2026-09-26) - The first release, a prerelease. 32 nodes in eight categories - Mapsui,
      Mapsui.Layers, Mapsui.Styles, Mapsui.Navigate, Mapsui.Project, Mapsui.Widgets, Mapsui.Skia,
      Mapsui.Debug - 244 tests, 19 help patches, and F1 on every node opens one. Tile layers
      (OpenStreetMap, any XYZ service) with a shared disk cache, geometry and feature layers, a
      graticule, five styles, navigation, picking, pixel/degree conversion and widgets. Wraps
      Mapsui 4.1.9; verified in vvvv gamma 7.4. Fetching is off until you switch it on. Not ready
      for real work: the node surface may still change between prereleases.
    </releaseNotes>
    <copyright>Copyright 2026 VL.Mapsui Contributors</copyright>
    <!-- The "VL" tag is what makes the pack discoverable at https://vvvv.org/packs -->
    <tags>VL vvvv gamma map mapsui gis openstreetmap tiles skia</tags>
    <repository type="git" url="https://github.com/rednotfound/VL.Mapsui" />

    <!--
      These must match the NugetDependency lines in VL.Mapsui.vl. The .vl is what teaches VL
      the types, so a node whose signature mentions Mapsui.Map only exists because Mapsui is
      declared there; the nuspec is what makes the package resolvable for a consumer.

      VL.Core, VL.Core.Skia, VL.CoreLib, VL.Skia and SkiaSharp are deliberately absent: they
      ship inside vvvv, which substitutes its own copies for anything declared here.
    -->
    <dependencies>
      <group targetFramework="net8.0">
        <dependency id="Mapsui"                version="4.1.9" />
        <dependency id="Mapsui.Tiling"         version="4.1.9" />
        <dependency id="NetTopologySuite"      version="2.6.0" />
        <dependency id="NetTopologySuite.Features" version="2.1.0" />
        <!--
          The package that MAKES geometry, which this one only draws. Declared so that installing
          VL.Mapsui brings it along and the help patch that draws a shape opens working: vvvv does
          not fetch a missing document dependency by itself - it shows the entry red and offers to
          install on rightclick.

          vvvv's own packs get away without this (VL.Skia's help uses VL.ImGui, VL.Audio declares no
          VL dependency at all and its help uses VL.Stride) because everything there ships inside
          vvvv and is therefore always present. Nothing guarantees that for us.
        -->
        <dependency id="VL.NetTopologySuite"   version="0.0.2-alpha" />
        <dependency id="Mapsui.Rendering.Skia" version="4.1.9" />
      </group>
    </dependencies>
  </metadata>

  <files>
    <!-- Entry point. Must sit at the package root, no subfolder. -->
    <file src="VL.Mapsui.vl" target="" />

    <file src="src\VL.Mapsui\bin\Release\net8.0\VL.Mapsui.dll" target="lib\net8.0\" />
    <file src="src\VL.Mapsui\bin\Release\net8.0\VL.Mapsui.xml" target="lib\net8.0\" />

    <!-- This package's own examples. target stays help\ because that is where vvvv looks. -->
    <file src="help\VL.Mapsui\**\*.vl" target="help\" />

    <!--
      Help.xml orders the help patches in the browser and carries their search tags. It needs its
      own line: the glob above takes .vl and nothing else, so leaving this out would ship the
      patches unordered and untagged with no warning anywhere.
    -->
    <file src="help\VL.Mapsui\Help.xml" target="help\" />

    <!--
      README.md is also the nuget.org page (<readme> above). NOTES.md is deliberately NOT shipped:
      it is a 2000-line working log of this repository, full of machine paths, and a user reading
      the package wants the design, not the forensics.
    -->
    <file src="README.md" target="docs\" />
    <file src="docs\ARCHITECTURE.md" target="docs\" />
    <file src="docs\MAPSUI-SURFACE.md" target="docs\" />
  </files>
</package>
